From f73d7e3104313c8627ae79e67ca1214ab0e2c74e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:26:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 38/61] linux-graphics

WebGL in a Linux build presenting Windows (see the user-agent commit)
reports what ANGLE's Direct3D 11 backend reports on Windows in place of the
host's (SwiftShader on a server): the limits, ranges and shader precisions
getParameter() and getShaderPrecisionFormat() return, without the ETC and
ASTC compressed texture extensions, and one common Intel integrated GPU's
names (Iris Xe, 0x9A49, the most common in the pool) unless a persona claims
another (platform/graphics/gpu/ windows_gpu_profile.h). WebGPU likewise: a
GPU-less server offers the SwiftShader adapter without
--enable-unsafe-webgpu (the GPU process lets the CPU adapter past the safety
level and the blocklist, and receives --use-chromium-defaults), and pages
see a Windows machine's GPU: its vendor and architecture (intel gen-12-lp by
default, a persona's through webgpu-spoof), its Direct3D 12 subgroup sizes,
no fallback adapter, the Direct3D 12 features and Chrome's tiered limits
measured on Windows, and bgra8unorm as the preferred canvas format.
WEBGL_debug_shaders' getTranslatedShaderSource() answers with the HLSL
Chrome's Direct3D 11 back end produces (the GPU process translates the
shader with ANGLE's own HLSL translator, angle_enable_hlsl, with that back
end's options and limits and the claimed vendor's workarounds), not ANGLE
Vulkan's SPIR-V. It translates the source the shader was compiled with,
with the shader extensions enabled by then (Windows defines an
extension's macro once the page enables it, and the external-image ones,
which Chrome's decoder requests, always), as ANGLE on Windows translates
at compile time, and the translated-length query answers for the same
text. With GL disabled (no usable GPU), a WebGL creation error
names no device, "Disabled" GL strings and a sandboxed GPU process, as
Chrome on Windows writes it, not the host's PCI display device (or
"Sandboxed = no" in a container run with --no-sandbox)
(content/renderer/renderer_blink_platform_impl.cc). Only what pages read
changes; the limits Blink and the GPU process enforce stay the host's.
Nothing changes on Windows or with --use-chromium-defaults.
